[Music]  a lot of my work is around corporate  income shifting which is one way that  companies respond to tax incentives  globally so if you think of a company  that has all of its income across all of  its operations around the world  it's going to be taxed on that income  and each jurisdiction and so if there  are different tax rates in different  jurisdictions which we know there are  then it's in the best interests of the  company from a tax planning perspective  to try to report more profits in low-tax  jurisdictions and less profits and high  tax jurisdictions  I like to think of it as first and  foremost an operational decision so  where as a company do you put your  footprint around the world such that  you're creating value in different  jurisdictions and this is something that  gets a lot of attention in particular  with intellectual property patents and  things like that  putting those in low tax jurisdictions  sometimes called tax havens and that  allows a lot of the profits to move be  reported in those low tax jurisdictions  but it's it's a broader analysis than  just intellectual property it's all of  the functions risks and assets of the  company and so the operational decision  is figuring out a tax efficient  footprint and then on top of that you  can layer an accounting decision and  that is now that I put some patents in  say Ireland how much of a royalty do all  of the other parts of the company have  to pay to my Irish subsidiary for the  right to exploit that intellectual  property and that fundamentally is it's  an economic question but it gets into  the accounting space of exactly how we  want to assign a price called a transfer  price between these two subsidiaries and  where does that allow us to report our  profits from a tax perspective  there's a global set of rules and each  country has its own set of rules - for  how you're supposed to come up with what  that price is  and the fundamental principle that we  operate in globally not all countries  but most countries these days is called  the arms-length standard so it's  supposed to be a price that you would  observe out on the open market between  two completely unrelated parties so if  you and I were transacting for the sale  of shoes or whatever it is right that  would be the price as long as it's at a  similar level of the market so wholesale  versus retail in a similar economy you  can take into account nuances like  developing economies and foreign  exchange rates and things like that but  it's supposed to be as if we're  operating at arm's length  the problem with that is that a lot of  these services and products that  companies are transacting with it  amongst their own subsidiaries are not  the same types of things that unrelated  parties transact so it's not often the  case that a company that exploits its  own intellectual property around the  world is going to also license that out  to unrelated parties as one example  another example is you think of a CEO  providing high-level strategic  management for all of the operations  worldwide that person is not also  providing those services for competitors  for example so it can be very difficult  to observe an arm's length price and  that's what provides a lot of discretion  to companies to try to establish a price  substantiate it defend it but also a  price that's going to work for best for  them from a tax perspective and this is  the big question that we're seeing these  days internationally a fundamental  question about transfer pricing should  arms-length be the right standard or  should it be more closely aligned with  where the physical footprint of the firm  is so discounting intellectual property  a little bit from from how much it  matters today and focusing more on where  sales are made where the customers are  and where the actual employees are  it seems tempting I think for countries  to set their tax policies in a way to  try to mitigate this on a unilateral  basis you know or they're acting in  their own best interests  but given globalization and just the  increasing amount of dealings that are  happening across borders I don't think  we get to a long-lasting answer until we  get countries cooperating trying to  harmonize tax laws it doesn't have to be  the same rate but at a minimum we need  to agree on how these cross-border  transactions should be priced where  profit should be recognized and until we  do that you're going to have companies  and individuals taking advantage of  differences  there's a lot of research indicating  that companies are shifting income they  are making operational and accounting  decisions to move income out of high tax  locations and into low tax jurisdictions  what's interesting about one of the  studies I have is that a lot of the  other research out there ignores one  major tax strategy that companies can  take and that is taking advantage of  their subsidiaries with losses which is  a really really important tax strategy  to companies when we include this  important strategy we find that the size  of the effect is actually much larger  than we thought before actually all of  the research out there could  underestimate the amount that  corporations are engaging in this income  shifting activity by as much as 50  percent which is a very big difference  so there's a lot more of it going on  than we thought and it's a lot bigger  than we thought  what does it mean to pay your fair share  whether it's a corporation or an  individual what does that look like what  does it look like to be a good corporate  citizen or a good citizen of of your  country where you owe taxes and it's a  tough question  because companies have a profit motive  they have shareholders that they're  beholden to and there's a perspective  the shareholder perspective of the firm  that companies should be maximizing  value for those shareholders there's  also a stakeholder perspective which is  that we're all stakeholders in this  society that we've built and so we  should all pay our fair share and I  think the two perspectives aren't  necessarily mutually exclusive but it's  very difficult to answer the question  what is fair because it's inherently  subjective if we know that companies are  doing this and we can estimate how much  they're doing it and we're starting to  look at what some of the consequences of  that is it's natural to ask what should  the policies be to think about what  could be done to mitigate the behavior  if we think it should be mitigated or  just generally you know how should  corporations be responding is there a  policy tool that we can use to try to  get that response instead of what  they're doing now  [Music]  you  [Music]  you

